Q150 nMOS can pull WP# pin of flash LOW. 


J150 pin header 2P (write protect flash, reset fpga combi)

	One of the PINS goes to GND through a resistor.

	Other side is connected to the PROG_B pin of FPGA, together with:
	- STM32's x pin thru 100R.
	- fpga prog -> R173 (10k) -> 3v3 side of R174 (1k) -> WP pin of flash.

  Which means, the jumper (or STM) can pull pull fpga's prog pin DOWN, which normally would be PULLED UP.
  thus, while PROG is high, WP of flash is pulled DOWN (by mosfet), which protects the FLASH from being written.
  "allowing" the WP to be HIGH, allows flash to be edited, which implies FPGA to be in a RESET state (Prog low).

  Thus summary again, prog b low, fpga in reset, flash editable. Can be done via short, or STM32 port tied LOW.


